2. Obtain the Access Token
You can obtain the access token using OAuth 2.0 by following these steps:

Using Postman:
Create a New Request:
Set the request type to POST.
Use the following URL (replace <domain> with your Salesforce domain, typically login.salesforce.com for production or test.salesforce.com for sandbox):
bash
Copy code
https://<domain>/services/oauth2/token
Set the Body Parameters:
Set the request body type to x-www-form-urlencoded.
Add the following parameters:
grant_type: password
client_id: <Consumer Key from Connected App>
client_secret: <Consumer Secret from Connected App>
username: <Salesforce Username>
password: <Salesforce Password + Security Token> (If a security token is required, append it to your password)
Send the Request:
Send the request, and if everything is correct, you will receive a JSON response with an access_token.
Using Code:
Node.js (Axios example):
javascript
Copy code
const axios = require('axios');

const getToken = async () => {
  try {
    const response = await axios.post('https://<domain>/services/oauth2/token', null, {
      params: {
        grant_type: 'password',
        client_id: '<Consumer Key>',
        client_secret: '<Consumer Secret>',
        username: '<Salesforce Username>',
        password: '<Salesforce Password + Security Token>',
      },
    });

    const { access_token } = response.data;
    console.log('Access Token:', access_token);
    return access_token;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error getting access token:', error.response.data);
  }
};

getToken();
3. Using the Access Token with Salesforce APIs
Once you have the access token, you can make requests to Salesforce APIs.
Example: Get Account Records
Endpoint: https://<domain>/services/data/v57.0/sobjects/Account

Method: GET

Headers:

json
Copy code
{
  "Authorization": "Bearer <Access Token>",
  "Content-Type": "application/json"
}
Node.js Example:

javascript
Copy code
const getAccounts = async (accessToken) => {
  try {
    const response = await axios.get('https://<domain>/services/data/v57.0/sobjects/Account', {
      headers: {
        Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`,
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      },
    });

    console.log('Accounts:', response.data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching accounts:', error.response.data);
  }
};

getToken().then(getAccounts);
4. Refreshing the Access Token
Access tokens expire after a certain period. You can use the refresh token (if you've requested one) to get a new access token.
To refresh the token, send a POST request to the same token endpoint, but use grant_type=refresh_token and include the refresh_token in the body.
This process allows you to authenticate and interact with Salesforce APIs securely.
